<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Dress for the Weather. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About the Weather</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Dress for the Weather.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Fill in blanks in sentences using a word bank related to a familiar context. Interactive: Write simple words supported with drawings to peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can fill in blanks in sentences about the weather using a word bank (e.g., selecting "rainy" or "sunny" to complete "Today is a ___ day."). Can write simple, weather-related words supported with drawings to communicate ideas (e.g., drawing a rain storm and writes, "rainy"). Can express simple weather-related ideas with guidance (e.g., "I like rainy weather.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of prior knowledge related to weather and clothing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up weather flashcards (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and asks, "What's the weather like today?" Students respond chorally or individually. The teacher then shows clothing flashcards (coat, hat, umbrella, boots) and asks, "What do you wear on a [sunny/rainy] day?" Students say words. The teacher introduces the idea of writing about the weather.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple sentence on the board: "Today is a sunny day." and "I wear a hat." The teacher points to each word as they write, saying it aloud clearly, emphasizing /s/ in "sunny" and initial /w/ in "wear". The teacher draws a sun next to the sentence. Then, the teacher models writing "rainy" and drawing rain, emphasizing the /r/ sound. The teacher shows how to use a word bank by circling "sunny" and "rainy" from a short list.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'sunny' a day or a hat?" (Expected: A day)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If it's rainy, do I need a coat or a sun?" (Expected: A coat)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About the Weather".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated paragraph or dialogue displayed on a chart or projector. Example: "Today is a sunny day. I like sunny days. I wear a hat and play outside. What is the weather like? It's rainy. I need my umbrella. I like rainy weather." The text uses simple present tense and adjectives + nouns.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing the weather and activities: "It's a sunny day, and I play outside." Responding to questions: "What's the weather like?" "It's rainy. I need my umbrella." Expressing preferences: "I like sunny days."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the text aloud, pointing to words. Students repeat key weather words (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and clothing items (hat, umbrella, coat, boots).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match weather pictures (sun, rain cloud, wind) to the corresponding word on the chart by pointing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks simple gist questions like, "What kind of day is the first part about?" (Expected: Sunny day). "What do I need for rainy weather?" (Expected: Umbrella).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing, using target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Adjectives + nouns (e.g., "rainy day"), Present simple (e.g., "I like sunny days."), use of weather (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and clothing vocabulary (coat, hat, umbrella, boots, scarf, jacket).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Fill-in-the-blanks):</b> Students receive a worksheet with incomplete sentences and a word bank (e.g., sunny, rainy, coat, umbrella, like). Sentences include: "Today is a ___ day." "I wear a ___." "I ___ cloudy weather." Students choose the correct word from the bank to fill in each blank. The teacher circulates, providing support and checking spelling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-Controlled Writing (Sentence Building with Pictures):</b> The teacher displays various large weather pictures (sun, rain, wind, cloud). For each picture, students write two simple sentences. For example, for a picture of a sun, students write: "It is a sunny day." and "I like sunny weather." For a picture of rain, they write: "It is a rainy day." "I wear a coat." The teacher guides them to use adjectives + nouns and present simple. Emphasis on /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "rainy." For "windy", the teacher reminds students of the initial /w/ and final /i/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interactive Writing (Peer Support with Drawings):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student draws a simple picture of one type of weather (e.g., a big cloud) and writes one weather word below it (e.g., "cloudy"). They exchange drawings with their partner. The partner then writes a sentence related to the drawing and word, for example, "Today is a cloudy day." or "I need a jacket." The teacher encourages peers to check each other's words and sentences for clarity and basic spelling.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All About the Weather".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual "My Weather Report" creative writing and drawing activity, followed by peer sharing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student receives a blank sheet of paper divided into two sections: a large drawing box and lines for writing below.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to "Draw your favorite weather and what you wear." (e.g., sunny day with a hat, rainy day with an umbrella).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below their drawing, students write 2-3 simple sentences to describe their drawing, using the target vocabulary and grammar. Example prompts on the board: "Today is a ___ day." "I like ___ weather." "I wear my ___." The teacher reminds students to use the word "and" to connect ideas (e.g., "I wear my boots and my jacket.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After writing, students briefly show their "Weather Report" to a classmate and read their sentences aloud. The classmate listens and can ask, "What is the weather like?" or "What do you wear?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of students' writing and understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist (for use of target vocabulary and simple sentence structure) and peer assessment (simple check for clarity of drawing and written word).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Weather Report" drawings and written sentences from the Production stage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., sunny, rainy, cold, warm, hat, umbrella, coat, boots).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Simple sentence structure:</b> (e.g., "Today is a ___ day.", "I wear a ___.", "I like ___ weather.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and legibility of writing:</b> (Words are readable and correspond to the drawing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Effort in drawing:</b> (Supports the written description).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What weather word did I write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I write a sentence about what I like to wear?" (Yes/No with a happy/sad face option)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson, and connection to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new weather word did you write today?" (e.g., windy, cloudy)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What sentence about weather did you like to write the most?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., Students were engaged with drawing and writing, word banks were helpful).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., More individual practice writing full sentences without a word bank; review spelling of common weather adjectives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson: The next lesson (Mediation & Integration) will build on these written descriptions. Students will use their "My Weather Reports" to present to the class and discuss what they learned about describing weather and clothing.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>